

[38;5;231m⏺[39m That answer changes the classification, so I'm stepping up the path — and I want to be explicit about why rather than
  quietly doing more work than you asked for.

  "Works across the app, persists, and other forms will need it later" is not a parameter on one function. It's a shared
  identity that outlives a single call and has consumers that don't exist yet — storage, a module that owns it, and a
  contract those future forms read. This repo has none of that today: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a single stub with no state, no storage,
  and no module boundaries ([38;5;153msrc/[39m is an unrelated [38;5;153mgreet[39m utility). Per the one-way ratchet, hidden complexity upgrades
  the path and nothing downgrades it, so [1mthis is now architectural[0m: clarifying questions, approaches with trade-offs, a
  written spec for you to review, then an implementation plan. The original one-line edit would have painted you into a
  corner the moment the second form showed up.

  First question, and this one drives most of the rest.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this identity is [3mtrusted[0m or merely [3mdescriptive[0m. A value the client generates and stores is fine for
  stitching log lines together and costs almost nothing; it is worthless for deciding what a user is allowed to see,
  because the client can set it to anything. A server-issued session identity can do both, but it means the login stub
  has to become a real request/response contract, and persistence has to be chosen with security in mind (cookie flags
  vs. [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m). Getting this wrong is expensive to reverse — every consumer you add later reads whichever shape you
  pick, and retrofitting trust onto a client-generated id means changing all of them.
